window.docContents[5].resolve({"/#monads-arithmetic-example-exercise-trace":{"contents":"The  type can be used with the evaluator to add optional tracing of some operations.\nIn particular, the type  can serve as a signal to trace a given operator:\n\n\n\nFor the tracing evaluator, expressions should have type .\nThis says that the operators in the expression consist of addition, subtraction, and multiplication, augmented with traced versions of each. The innermost argument is  to signal that there are no further special operators inside of , only the three basic ones.\n\nDo the following:\n\n1. Implement a  instance2. Write an  function to apply traced operators to their arguments, logging both the operator and the arguments, with type \n\nIf the exercise has been completed correctly, then\n\n\n\nshould result in\n\n\n\nHint: values of type  will appear in the resulting log. In order to display them as a result of #eval, the following instances are required:\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monads\u0009Example: Arithmetic in Monads\u0009Further Effects\u0009Exercises","header":"4.3.3.4.3. A Tracing Evaluator","id":"/#monads-arithmetic-example-exercise-trace"}});